= Machine learning =

== Overview ==
'''Machine learning''' is a field of computer science concerned with computational methods that improve or adapt task performance using data or experience rather than relying only on explicitly hand-written task rules.

Machine learning includes many paradigms, such as supervised learning, unsupervised learning, self-supervised learning, reinforcement learning, online learning, and representation learning.

== Learning from data ==
A learning algorithm maps observed data and assumptions into a model, representation, policy, or decision rule.

The learned object can then be used for prediction, classification, generation, control, ranking, clustering, or other tasks.

== Machine learning and artificial intelligence ==
Machine learning is a major method family within artificial intelligence, but the terms are not synonyms.

Artificial intelligence also includes symbolic reasoning, planning, search, knowledge representation, optimization, and other methods that need not learn from data.

== Machine learning and biological learning ==
[[Learning]] in cognitive science concerns changes in biological or cognitive systems through experience.

Machine learning is computational and mathematical.

The fields can inspire one another, but:

<pre>
machine learning ≠ biological learning
</pre>

== Generalization ==
A central goal is [[Generalization (machine learning)|generalization]]: performing well on new examples rather than only reproducing behavior on the training data.

Generalization depends on data, model class, optimization, inductive bias, regularization, evaluation design, and the relationship between training and deployment distributions.

== Related concepts ==
[[Inductive bias]], [[Training set]], [[Validation set]], [[Test set]], [[Overfitting]], [[Underfitting]], [[Distribution shift]], [[Probability]], and [[Optimization]] are central neighboring concepts.

